1
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:10,000
Imagine standing in front of an army so massive... that its soldiers seem to cover the entire horizon.

2
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:13,700
Behind you... there is nowhere to run.

3
00:00:14,340 --> 00:00:18,200
In front of you... an empire that wants to conquer your homeland.

4
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:23,000
And standing between them... just a few thousand Greek warriors.

5
00:00:23,430 --> 00:00:26,000
Among them... three hundred Spartans.

6
00:00:26,510 --> 00:00:28,600
They knew they might never return home.

7
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:30,900
But they had made their decision.

8
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:34,000
The year was 480 BC.

9
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:40,400
Across the sea, one of the largest armies the ancient world had ever seen was on the move.

10
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:47,000
And in a narrow pass in central Greece, a small group of men prepared to stand in its way.

11
00:00:47,790 --> 00:00:52,900
What happened over the next three days would echo for more than two thousand years.

12
00:00:53,690 --> 00:00:57,200
This... is the story of the Battle of Thermopylae.

13
00:00:57,380 --> 00:01:04,100
In 480 BC, the Persian Empire was one of the most powerful empires in the ancient world.

14
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:09,900
It stretched across Asia and beyond, with soldiers from many different nations.

15
00:01:10,580 --> 00:01:12,500
But one region still resisted.

16
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:13,900
Greece.

17
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:18,900
The Persian kings had built their empire by conquering kingdom after kingdom...

18
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:23,300
from Egypt in the west to the borders of India in the east.

19
00:01:24,090 --> 00:01:30,300
Conquered peoples were asked to offer "earth and water", a symbol of submission to the Great King.

20
00:01:30,980 --> 00:01:32,200
Many cities agreed.

21
00:01:32,580 --> 00:01:37,400
But years earlier, when Persian messengers came to Athens and Sparta,

22
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:41,700
ancient writers say they were thrown into a pit and a well...

23
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:45,800
and told to find their earth and water down there.

24
00:01:46,630 --> 00:01:51,500
Ten years earlier, the Persians had been defeated at the Battle of Marathon.

25
00:01:52,120 --> 00:01:57,400
Now, King Xerxes the First wanted revenge... and control.

26
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:02,100
He gathered an enormous army... and marched toward Greece.

27
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:07,500
The ancient historian Herodotus claimed this army numbered in the millions.

28
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:12,300
Modern historians believe that number is greatly exaggerated.

29
00:02:12,930 --> 00:02:15,400
Perhaps one to three hundred thousand men.

30
00:02:16,150 --> 00:02:18,400
But even that was an overwhelming force.

31
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:22,900
To move it, Xerxes built bridges of boats across the Hellespont,

32
00:02:23,390 --> 00:02:26,000
the narrow strait between Asia and Europe.

